// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`fix prompt rendering > renders the dead-code-cleanup prompt with required fields 1`] = `
"# Dead-code cleanup task

A \`knip\` \`unused-export\`/\`unused-type\` finding means the export is unnecessary, NOT that the
symbol is dead. A symbol is still live if it's reached dynamically — public API, reflection/DI,
config-loaded, or non-static imports — even when knip reports it unused; never delete those.
Otherwise check the contents below for in-file references:

- Referenced in-file → remove only the \`export\` keyword (and any re-export/barrel entry); keep
  the declaration. Deleting it would break typecheck.
- Zero in-file references → delete the whole declaration (and its re-export). For an unused
  \`default\` export still used in-file, drop the \`default export\` modifiers and keep the binding.
- When unsure a reference exists, prefer dropping the \`export\` over deleting.

<file_contents>
(file content not supplied — read the file before editing)
</file_contents>

<findings>
\`\`\`json
[
  {
    "file": "src/unused.ts",
    "range": {
      "startLine": 1,
      "startCol": 0,
      "endLine": 1,
      "endCol": 10
    },
    "tool": "knip",
    "rule": "unused-export",
    "category": "dead-code",
    "severity": "error",
    "message": "Unused export: unusedHelper"
  }
]
\`\`\`
</findings>

<editable_files>
- src/unused.ts
</editable_files>

Done when these verification targets no longer report the dead-code findings:
<verification_targets>
- src/unused.ts
</verification_targets>"
`;

exports[`fix prompt rendering > renders the generated-source-repair prompt with required fields 1`] = `
"# Generated-source repair task

The finding is in a generated artifact that is rebuilt from source. Fix the source owner, never a
generated file under \`dist\`/\`build\`/\`out\`/\`.next\` or generated client output — the build
overwrites those.

<file_contents>
(file content not supplied — read the file before editing)
</file_contents>

<findings>
\`\`\`json
[
  {
    "file": "dist/client.js",
    "range": {
      "startLine": 1,
      "startCol": 0,
      "endLine": 1,
      "endCol": 10
    },
    "tool": "sonarjs",
    "rule": "no-identical-expressions",
    "category": "bug",
    "severity": "error",
    "message": "Generated artifact finding"
  }
]
\`\`\`
</findings>

<editable_files>
- src/client.ts
</editable_files>

Done when, after the build regenerates the artifact, these verification targets no longer report
the findings:
<verification_targets>
- dist/client.js
- src/client.ts
</verification_targets>"
`;

exports[`fix prompt rendering > renders the multi-file-duplicate-refactor prompt with required fields 1`] = `
"# Cross-file duplicate refactor task

Remove the jscpd duplicate code from EVERY clone site (each finding's \`flowPath\` gives the
regions) — editing only one site leaves the finding standing. State the clone type, then extract:

- Exact clone → move the shared logic to one file, export it, import it in the other.
- Renamed/parameterized → extract a function taking the varying parts as parameters.
- Gapped → extract the shared core; pass the varying parts as a callback or options.

If an empty shared-module path is listed as editable, you may create it and put the extracted
function there. If the clones differ too much for a clean shared function, make one file the
canonical owner and import from it — don't add boolean/mode flags to bridge callers. For test
clones, extract a simple factory helper. Delete a clone only if it is provably dead code.

<file_contents>
(file content not supplied — read the file before editing)
</file_contents>

<findings>
\`\`\`json
[
  {
    "file": "src/a.ts",
    "range": {
      "startLine": 10,
      "startCol": 1,
      "endLine": 20,
      "endCol": 2
    },
    "tool": "jscpd",
    "rule": "duplicate-code",
    "category": "duplication",
    "severity": "error",
    "message": "Identical sub-expressions",
    "flowPath": [
      {
        "file": "src/a.ts",
        "line": 10,
        "range": {
          "startLine": 10,
          "startCol": 1,
          "endLine": 20,
          "endCol": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "file": "src/b.ts",
        "line": 30,
        "range": {
          "startLine": 30,
          "startCol": 1,
          "endLine": 40,
          "endCol": 2
        }
      }
    ]
  }
]
\`\`\`
</findings>

<editable_files>
- src/a.ts
- src/b.ts
</editable_files>

Example — \`a.ts\` and \`b.ts\` both reduce over different field names; extract once and import:
<example>
export function sumLineItems(lines: { price: number; qty: number }[]) {
  return lines.reduce((s, l) => s + l.price * l.qty, 0);
}
</example>

Done when the duplication is gone from these verification targets and imports/exports stay valid:
<verification_targets>
- src/a.ts
- src/b.ts
</verification_targets>"
`;

